course: dictionary
lesson: 5901
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The word marble is a good example. Read the words around it, because they show which meaning the writer wants. «Tom and Ann have 40 marbles.» In a counting problem, marbles are small balls you can count.
explain: «For example, a girl in Texas finds a marble on the rug where her baby brother crawls; she picks it up and puts it high on a shelf before he can reach it.» A tiny glass ball is small enough to pick up and hide on a shelf, so the clues point to the glass ball, not the stone.
explain: The same word can also name a hard stone. Marble is smooth and often white with lines, so people carve it into statues. When a word has two meanings, pick one, put it into the sentence in place of the hard word, and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some words belong to jokes. «Lose your marbles» does not mean you have dropped your little glass balls. It is a joke that means going a bit crazy. The whole sentence and its feeling will help you decide.
fact: Marble is a noun. Its plural is marbles.
fact: A marble is a small glass ball for playing games.
fact: Marble is a hard, smooth stone, often white with lines, used for statues and buildings.
fact: «Marbles» is also a game played by rolling marbles.
fact: In science, marble is a kind of rock made deep in the earth.
exercise: «Tom and Ann have 40 marbles.» In this sentence, what are marbles? | A joke about going crazy | A rock made deep in the earth | *Small glass balls for playing games | Hard, smooth stone for statues
exercise: «The statue was carved from white marble.» What does marble mean here? | *A hard, smooth stone used for statues | A game played in the playground | Going a bit crazy | A small glass ball
exercise: The phrase «lose your marbles» means | Winning a big prize | Playing a game badly | *Going a bit crazy | Dropping a jar of glass balls
test: In science class, what is marble? | *A kind of rock made deep in the earth | A game played in the playground | Going a bit crazy | A small glass ball for playing
test: In «3 bags of 10 marbles and 4 loose marbles are 30 + 4 = 34 marbles», what does marbles mean? | A game played by rolling | A rock made deep in the earth | *Counted small balls | Hard, smooth stones for statues
exam: Which tip helps you choose the right meaning of marble? | Ignore the sentence and guess | Always pick the first meaning in the dictionary | *Read the words around it and swap each meaning into the sentence | Skip every word that has more than one meaning
